    "https://faculty.uic.edu/hr/": {
      "title": "Policies",
      "description": "Policies | Office of the Vice Provost for Faculty Affairs | University of Illinois Chicago University of Illinois Chicago Search the site Toggle Menu Search University of Illinois Chicago Office of the Vice Provost for Faculty Affairs UIC.edu Campus Map Office of the Provost CATE IDMPS Faculty Handbook Search For Faculty Expand For Faculty menu Advancement Programs Emeriti Faculty Faculty Resources Promotion & Tenure (P&T) Teaching & Learning For Administrators Expand For Administrators menu Administrator Toolkits Faculty Hiring Toolkit Leadership Development Promotion & Tenure (P&T) Student Evaluation of Teaching Program (SETP) Webguide To Policies & Procedures Recognition Expand Recognition menu Celebrating Excellence OVPFA Awards UIC Awards National & International Awards (Non-UIC) UIC Faculty Publications Policies Events Expand Events menu Faculty Awards Ceremony & Reception Orientation Workshops Events Beyond OVPFA News Expand News menu Newsletter Archives OVPFA News About Expand About menu Contact Org Charts Staff Directory Office of the Vice Provost for Faculty Affairs View Menu Down arrow icon Policies intro Copy link Below are links to the relevant policies and procedures related to Faculty Affairs. Human Resource professionals who are responsible for completing the required campus-level approval process will find all necessary information below and in the For Administrators section of the site. UIC Faculty Handbook Appointment Types and Appointment Processing Copy link Policies in this section pertain to faculty hiring, contracts, agreements, recruitment, retention, and additional appointments to current ones. The range of policies include requirements for hiring tenure system, non-tenure, and faculty administrative positions, recruiting under-represented faculty and dual-career academic couples/partners, and appointing distinguished professors, named chairs and professors, and for summer employment. Appointment Types and Appointment Processing (Section FPP 100) Appointment Types and Appointment Processing (Section FPP 100) 101 Review and Approval of Offers With Indefinite Tenure 102 Multi-Year Contracts for Non-Tenured Faculty 104 Partner Accommodation Program Policy 105 Guidelines for Appointing and Investing Named Chairs and Professorships 106 Guidelines for Appointing Tenured and Tenure-Track Faculty 107 Guidelines for Appointing Non-Tenured Faculty Adoption of Teaching Modifier for Non-Tenured Faculty Guidance for Teaching Modifier 108 Guidelines for Appointing “Q” Probationary Faculty 109 Guidelines for Appointing Faculty Under a “W” Agreement 110 Guidelines for Summer Appointments 111 Guidelines and Process for Appointment of Distinguished Professors 112 Guidelines for Appointing Hourly Faculty 113 Employment of Non-Native English Speaking Faculty Members 114 Guidelines for Faculty Administrative Job Descriptions 115 Instructional Faculty Qualifications Leaves Copy link Policies in this section pertain to faculty educational, paid, and unpaid leaves. Policies include requirements for sabbaticals, educational leaves, and leaves of absence without pay. Leaves (Section FPP 200) Leaves (Section FPP 200) 201 Sabbatical Leaves of Absence 202 Educational Leave 203 Leave of Absence Without Pay Probationary Period Copy link Policies in this section pertain to the tenure-track probationary period. Policies include requirements for a delayed start to the probationary period for tenure-track appointments beginning after the eighth week of the academic year, tenure holds and rollbacks, the Mid-Probationary Review, and switching from the tenure to the non-tenure track. Probationary Period (Section FPP 300) Probationary Period (Section FPP 300) 301 Mid-Probationary Review 302 Policy on Interruptions of the Probationary Period (Tenure Rollback) 303 Tenure Hold Policy 304 Track Switch Policy 305 Tenure Code Provision with Initial Partial Term Appointment Family-Work Friendly Policies Copy link Policies in this section pertain to faculty modified duties due to life changing events and remote work. Family-Work Friendly Policies (Section FPP 500) Family-Work Friendly Policies (Section FPP 500) 501 Modified Teaching Duties for Faculty Members with Life-Changing Events 502 Faculty Remote Work Salary and Compensation Copy link Policies in this section pertain to faculty compensation for services beyond full-time and mid-year salary increases. Salary and Compensation (Section FPP 600) Salary and Compensation (Section FPP 600) 601 Compensation for Services Beyond Full-Time 602 Mid-Year Salary Increase Reviews and Development Copy link Policies in this section pertain to faculty performance and teaching evaluations, mentoring, and reviews for unit executive officers (heads, chairs, directors). Reviews and Development (Section FPP 700) Reviews and Development (Section FPP 700) 701 Annual Evaluation of Faculty at UIC 702 Teaching Evaluation of Faculty at UIC 702-1 Teaching Evaluation Special Amendment 703 UEO Fifth Year Reviews 706 Faculty Mentoring Policy Employee Relations Copy link Policies in this section pertain to faculty grievances, ability to work, sanctions, and dismissal of non-tenure system faculty. Employee Relations (Section FPP 800) Employee Relations (Section FPP 800) 801 Faculty Grievance Procedures 802 Sanctions Other Than Dismissal for Cause 803 Evaluation of Ability to Work 804 Dismissal of Non-Tenured Faculty w/Multi-Year Contract Retirement, Resignation, and Termination Copy link Policies in this section pertain to granting emeritus status, re-hiring retirees, and notice of non-reappointment. Retirement, Resignation, and Termination (Section FPP 900) Retirement, Resignation, and Termination (Section FPP 900) 901 Policy on Notice of Non-Reappointment 902 Granting Emeritus Status Policy 903 Re-Hire of University of Illinois Retirees Historical Policies Copy link Policies in this section are inactive and available for historical purposes. Historical Policies (Section FPP 1100) Historical Policies (Section FPP 1100) Service/Pay Changes 2003: Method of Final Payment/Service Teaching and Instruction (Section FPP 1200): 1201 Online Course Material for Fall 2020 Faculty Affairs Policy Infrastructure: Development to Enactment Copy link Faculty Affairs Policy Infrastructure: Development to Enactment University of Illinois Chicago Contact Office of the Vice Provost for Faculty Affairs (OVPFA) 601 S. Morgan St., 2731 UH Chicago, IL 60607 facultyaffairs@uic.edu Linked In UIC.edu Academic Calendar Athletics Campus Directory Disability Resources Emergency Information Event Calendar Job Openings Library Maps UIC Safe Mobile App UIC Today UI Health Veterans Affairs Report a Concern Powered by Red 3.0.51 This site is protected by reCAPTCHA and the Google Privacy Policy and Terms of Service apply. © 2026 The Board of Trustees of the University of Illinois | Privacy Statement University of Illinois System Urbana-Champaign Springfield Chicago X Cookie Notice We use Cookies on this site to enhance your experience and improve our marketing\n                        efforts. Click on “About Cookies” to learn more. By continuing to browse without\n                        changing your browser settings to block or delete Cookies, you agree to the storing of\n                        Cookies and related technologies on your device. University of Illinois System Cookie Policy. Link opens in a new window About Cookies Close Cookie Notice University of Illinois - Cookie Information About Cookies Cookies and related technologies (herein “Cookies”) are small text files that a website saves on\n                your computer when you visit the site. Cookies the University sets are called first-party\n                Cookies. The data collected might be about you, your device, your preferences, or your login\n                information. This data is mostly used to make the website work as expected so, for example, you\n                don’t have to keep re-entering your credentials whenever you come back to the site. Cookies set\n                by third parties are called third-party Cookies. We use third-party Cookies for analyzing\n                website traffic and our advertising and marketing efforts. We have divided the Cookies we use\n                into the following categories: Strictly Necessary, Performance, Functional, and Targeting. Under\n                each category heading below you will find a general description of the Cookies in each category.\n                You can change your browser settings to block, delete, or alert you to Cookies. The Help menu on\n                the menu bar of most browsers will tell you how to do that. However, if you do, you may have to\n                manually adjust preferences every time you visit a site and some features may not work as\n                intended. See the University of Illinois System Cookie Policy Link opens in a new window for more information. Strictly Necessary Cookies - Always Active Strictly Necessary Cookies are first-party Cookies that are necessary for the website to\n                    function. They can be either permanent or temporary and are usually only set in response to\n                    actions made directly by you that amount to a request for services, such as logging in or\n                    filling in forms. For example, we use Strictly Necessary Cookies to handle user registration\n                    and login. Some sites require the use of Strictly Necessary Cookies to access the site, such\n                    as University websites requiring University credentialed authentication. If you set your\n                    browser to block or delete Cookies, you may not be able to access the site or some parts of\n                    the site will not work. Performance Cookies - Always Active Performance Cookies allow us to count visits and traffic sources so we can measure and\n                    improve the performance and effectiveness of University websites. Performance Cookies also\n                    help the University understand which webpages are the most and least popular, see how\n                    visitors move around the site, and determine whether webpage content is relevant to user\n                    interests. Performance Cookies may be first-party or third party, permanent or temporary,\n                    and do not personally identify individual visitors. Some Performance Cookies are “analytics”\n                    Cookies (e.g., Google Analytics), using third-party software tools, which help us understand\n                    more about how our websites are used and where visitors come from by collecting and\n                    aggregating anonymous information on the pages visited and any advertisements viewed. The\n                    University does not take responsibility for the collection, use, and management of data by\n                    any third-party software tool provider unless required to do so by applicable law. If you\n                    set your browser to block or delete Cookies, some site services and functionalities may not\n                    work. Functional Cookies - Always Active Functional Cookies enhance the performance and functionality of our websites but are\n                    non-essential to their use. These permanent Cookies allow our website to remember\n                    information from your previous visits, such as details you submitted before or your\n                    previously stated preferences. These Cookies may also be used to provide services you\n                    request, such as newsletters or publications. They may be first- or third-party Cookies that\n                    enable services we have added to our webpages. If you set your browser to block or delete\n                    Cookies, some or all of these services may not function properly. Targeting Cookies - Always Active Targeting Cookies are used to deliver content tailored to your interests and may be\n                    temporary or permanent. They may also be first-party or third-party Cookies. Targeting\n                    Cookies are based on uniquely identifying your browser and device; they do not store\n                    information such as your name. The University may use targeting Cookies prepared by the\n                    University, its third-party contractors, or advertising partners to provide you with\n                    personalized University display advertising and promotional material about the University\n                    and its programs. The University may also allow third parties to place Cookies on your\n                    device that collect and use anonymous information about your visits to, and interactions\n                    with, our websites to personalize advertisements and promotional materials for University\n                    goods and services. Targeting Cookies may be used by our third-party contractors or our\n                    advertising partners to build a profile of your interests and show you relevant\n                    advertisements on other sites. We may share information about your use of our site with our\n                    social media, advertising, and analytics partners who may combine it with other information\n                    that you have provided to them or that they have collected from your use of their services.\n                    If you set your browser to block or delete Cookies, you will still see advertisements, but\n                    they will be less targeted to your interests. Close"
    }
